% ============================================================================
% friction-types.query.adj — a worked recall over the physics friction-types
% library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "when does static friction
% act?": it states no mechanics fact of its own — it only `import`s the grounded
% table and asks binding queries. The engine resolves each `?` against the
% `friction_context` rows and returns the context + the table's source/locator/
% trust. The fourth query runs the relation BACKWARD (bind the context
% `fluid_layers`, recall its friction type — fluid). A word that is not one of
% the four everyday friction types abstains honestly — the engine never invents
% a context.
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative import resolves):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli friction-types.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "friction-types.adj"

? friction_context(static, $Context)     % expect at_rest
? friction_context(rolling, $Context)    % expect spherical_object
? friction_context(fluid, $Context)      % expect fluid_layers
? friction_context($Friction, sliding_motion) % reverse bind — expect sliding
? friction_context(gravity, $Context)    % expect abstain — gravity is a force, not one of the four everyday friction types
